# fork #95: the live lineage's projection (`project:corpus`, #93 D1). It belongs in this container
# rather than in a new one for the reason this file's own first paragraph gives -- it makes no outside
# request, so it competes for no host budget and must not be stopped along with a collector that is
# being rolled back. Its arm in needs.pipeline_stage is `analyze` for the same reason: it writes a
# needs.analysis_run row, not a collector_health one.
#
# **Why hourly is permissible: the cadence buys latency and nothing else.** Every value this stage
# writes is read off the tubedepth row -- `collected_at` is the artifact's own fetch time, not this
# pass's clock -- every insert is ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, and a re-run adds only what was missing. So
# no frequency changes what a row says; it only changes how far behind the collector the corpus is.
# Hourly is one pass per flatten hour, and the daily analysis #96 lands then never reads a corpus more
# than an hour old.
#
# **Permissible is not measured. T for this line is unknown, and the first pass is not the steady
# state.** This file's rule is that the gap between two adjacent lines is wider than the earlier
# line's worst-case T. The `0 8` commentary below records its measured first-night T; this line
# needs the same evidence. The two numbers here are different by orders of magnitude, because a
# backlog collected before the 2026-08-25 cutover is sitting in tubedepth right now (measured on
# production 2026-09-12): video_snapshots 27,318 rows / 5,908 distinct videos, 4,683 on the active
# panel; listing_entries 166,957; comments 285,749, of which 239,487 hang on panel videos.
#   T_first   one pass over that backlog -- on the order of a quarter-million corpus_document rows
#             written in one go, paged and committed page by page.
#   T_steady  every pass after it -- one flatten hour's increment, writing almost nothing, and re-reading
#             the panel's videos and their comments to find that out.
# **The procedure, before this line is trusted:** the coordinator runs the first pass by hand from the
# upstream checkout, before the stack is recreated, and times it (T_first); then runs a second pass
# immediately after and times that (T_steady). This interval is confirmed or moved from those two
# numbers, and the measurement is written into this file beside #242's. Until then the hour is a
# choice, not a result.
# What an overrun costs is duplicated work, not data. This line takes **no** lock -- the analyze lock
# is one global lock and letting an hourly projection hold it would push the 05:00 pass off its own
# night -- and supercronic starts an overlapping line rather than skipping it. Two passes running at
# once therefore read the same rows and write them under the same ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, so the
# second one inserts nothing and costs a second read; the snapshot row's insert and its
# `least(collected_at, ...)` update are serialised by the row lock. The reason to measure T anyway is
# that an overrun this line pays for every hour is a host doing the same read forever.
#
# :41 is the phase. The flatten line runs at :07/:22/:37/:52 (stack/crontab.d/collector-youtube-flatten,
# phase 7), so :41 sits four minutes after a flatten tick and eleven before the next. Landing inside a
# flatten batch is harmless rather than something to schedule around -- the projection then sees fewer
# rows and the next hour picks up the rest -- so the phase is about not starting two statements against
# the same tables at the same instant, not about correctness.
